  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "For every natural number n, including n = 0, and every signed real-valued function f on the coordinate space Fin n → ℝ that is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable and has compact support, let γ be the product of n centered scalar Gaussian probability laws of variance one, and let D_i f(x) be the real Fréchet derivative of f at x applied to the coordinate vector with entry 1 at i and 0 elsewhere. The functions f², f² log(f²), every (D_i f)², and the finite coordinate sum Σ_i (D_i f)² are integrable with respect to γ. Writing A = ∫ f² dγ, one has ∫ f² log(f²) dγ − A log A ≤ 2 ∫ Σ_i (D_i f)² dγ. The logarithm is Lean Real.log, defined also at zero with log 0 = 0. No assumption A = 1 or positivity of f or A is imposed. At n = 0 the carrier is a singleton, γ is its probability law, the coordinate sum is empty and equals zero, and the entropy difference is zero. The zero function and zero square mass are included. The energy is the coordinate square sum, not the squared operator norm of the derivative for the default Pi supremum norm.",
